    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d743">

                <pb facs="0782" n="680" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg680"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.542/54</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in China (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1102</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-06-09">June 9, 1933</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Reference is made to your despatches Nos.
                    1937 of February 1, 1933, and 2035 of April 3, 1933, to your telegram No. 436,
                    May 15, 1933, 2 p.m. and to Shanghai’s despatch No. 7454 of February 6, 1933, to
                    the Legation, in regard to the protection in China of American patents.<anchor
                        n="67" xml:id="d743-f1"/></p>
                <p>You are directed to watch closely the situation in China as regards the
                    protection of American patents and, from time to time on appropriate occasions,
                    to impress on the Chinese authorities that the American Government expects the
                    Chinese Government to take adequate steps at an early date to enact the
                    necessary legislation for the protection of American patents in China.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d743-f1" n="67" xml:id="d743-f1-content">
                <p>Despatches not printed.</p>
